# PowerShell build scripts are COPY'd into Docker images; their byte content is
# the layer-cache key. Disable git line-ending conversion so a checkout can never
# silently flip LF<->CRLF and invalidate hours of cached build layers.
*.ps1 -text
*.psm1 -text
*.env -text
*.patch -text
# extensionless shell scripts shipped into images (MSYS sh chokes on CRLF)
makedef -text eol=lf
# Shell scripts must stay LF in the working tree on EVERY platform. They are
# sourced by bash - both inside the images and, on a Windows host, when this
# repo is bind-mounted into a Linux container (the documented Rancher Desktop
# workflow). A CRLF checkout makes bash choke on the trailing \r
# ("$'\r': command not found", "syntax error near unexpected token $'{\r'"),
# which silently defeats the logging/module bootstrap. Without this rule
# core.autocrlf=true flips all of them to CRLF on a Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
# Same layer-cache-key argument as the PowerShell rules above — these are all
# COPY'd into Windows images too, and were left unprotected until 2026-08-14
# (backlog #55). The gap was not theoretical: windows/scripts/patches/litert-lm/
# held ONE CRLF .cmake next to five LF siblings, so a fresh clone on any
# core.autocrlf=true host (this one; GitHub windows-latest) flipped bytes on the
# other five and busted media-litert plus everything downstream.
#   *.cmake  — patches/{litert-lm,iree}/*.cmake, injected into the build
#   *.cc/*.h — patches/litert-lm/*.cc and scripts/shims/*.h (compiled shims)
#   *.cmd    — scripts/entrypoint.cmd, COPY'd into the final image
*.cmake -text
*.cc -text
*.h -text
*.cmd -text
# PATH rules, deliberately last so they win: everything under these two trees is
# COPY'd or bind-mounted into a Windows image, so its bytes are a layer-cache
# key REGARDLESS of extension. The per-extension rules above miss whatever
# nobody thought of — .md, .py, .psd1 and .txt were all found unprotected here
# on 2026-08-14, and `COPY downloads C:\temp\downloads` means even
# downloads/README.md sits in a layer. A path rule needs no maintenance when a
# new file type shows up; Dockerfile.EolAttributes.Tests.ps1 enforces it.
# (No *.sh/*.bash exist under these trees, so the eol=lf rules above are not
# overridden. If one is ever added, re-check that ordering.)
windows/scripts/** -text
windows/downloads/** -text
